1
00:00:03,378 --> 00:00:04,421
Hello

2
00:00:04,421 --> 00:00:07,298
and welcome to our course
on the input metrics.

3
00:00:07,298 --> 00:00:10,427
I'm Bill Carr
and I spent 15 years at Amazon

4
00:00:10,719 --> 00:00:13,054
as the Vice president of digital media.

5
00:00:13,054 --> 00:00:14,139
I'm Colin Bryar.

6
00:00:14,139 --> 00:00:17,183
I spent a little over 12 years at Amazon
as a technical vice

7
00:00:17,183 --> 00:00:20,562
president, and two years as Jeff
Bezos's chief of staff.

8
00:00:21,021 --> 00:00:24,190
This course will enable you to achieve,
first,

9
00:00:24,774 --> 00:00:28,153
a deep understanding
of the concept of input metrics.

10
00:00:28,695 --> 00:00:31,156
Second, to identify input metrics

11
00:00:31,156 --> 00:00:34,159
for your business, product operation
or function.

12
00:00:34,868 --> 00:00:38,204
Third, to measure and analyze your inputs.

13
00:00:38,997 --> 00:00:42,208
Fourth, to be able to conduct
an effective weekly business review.

14
00:00:42,876 --> 00:00:46,337
And finally, fifth, to implement
and use the Weekly Business

15
00:00:46,337 --> 00:00:49,632
Review Reporting Tool
to visualize your metrics.

16
00:00:50,258 --> 00:00:51,676
For each lesson, we'll share.

17
00:00:51,676 --> 00:00:53,386
Real world examples to help

18
00:00:53,386 --> 00:00:57,098
you understand the practical applications
of these principles and practices.

19
00:00:57,557 --> 00:01:00,268
In addition, you'll have access
to our library of related

20
00:01:00,268 --> 00:01:03,897
documents, templates,
and tools to download, read, and use.

21
00:01:04,355 --> 00:01:08,276
Give a group of smart people
a big goal, such as an aggressive revenue

22
00:01:08,276 --> 00:01:11,279
target, and trust
they'll figure out how to do it.

23
00:01:11,613 --> 00:01:14,657
That's the way a lot of companies
try to optimize their business.

24
00:01:15,075 --> 00:01:17,786
And that's what we did at Amazon
in the early days, too.

25
00:01:17,786 --> 00:01:21,039
In 2000,
after roughly five years in business

26
00:01:21,039 --> 00:01:24,209
at Amazon, we realized that this method
wasn't working for us.

27
00:01:24,584 --> 00:01:26,544
The business was getting more complicated.

28
00:01:26,544 --> 00:01:29,839
We weren't serving our customers
well enough, and we were not achieving

29
00:01:29,839 --> 00:01:32,842
our desired outcomes
as consistent as we wanted.

30
00:01:32,926 --> 00:01:34,552
We had a choice to make.

31
00:01:34,552 --> 00:01:37,514
We could double down on our approach,
rely on good

32
00:01:37,514 --> 00:01:40,517
intentions, and ask people
to try harder to hit their goals.

33
00:01:41,559 --> 00:01:42,018
Or we could

34
00:01:42,018 --> 00:01:45,688
plot a new course of action,
one of process control.

35
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:50,318
The required us to learn new skills,
apply them rigorously and consistently.

36
00:01:50,443 --> 00:01:53,655
We chose this latter path
and it has paid dividends ever since.

37
00:01:54,322 --> 00:01:57,659
Every business can be viewed as a process
and processes

38
00:01:57,659 --> 00:01:59,702
have both inputs and outputs.

39
00:01:59,702 --> 00:02:01,913
We were too focused on the outputs.

40
00:02:01,913 --> 00:02:04,666
Moreover, a process like Amazon's
retail business

41
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:07,669
can be decomposed
into many smaller processes.

42
00:02:07,669 --> 00:02:10,713
Rather than ask teams
to optimize their output metrics

43
00:02:11,172 --> 00:02:13,550
and leave them up to their own devices.

44
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:16,553
We asked them to identify their outputs
and inputs.

45
00:02:16,719 --> 00:02:21,266
Then we gave them tools to reduce
the variation, improve the weekly average

46
00:02:21,266 --> 00:02:25,478
for their input metrics,
which in turn improves output metrics

47
00:02:25,728 --> 00:02:29,774
like active customers,
sales, revenue, and profit.

48
00:02:30,108 --> 00:02:32,944
So why should you care about input metrics
in this course?

49
00:02:32,944 --> 00:02:34,529
Well, there's two big reasons.

50
00:02:34,529 --> 00:02:36,739
First,
this course is going to give you knowledge

51
00:02:36,739 --> 00:02:39,909
and tools to gain
a better operational grasp of what drives

52
00:02:39,909 --> 00:02:42,912
your business
so you can achieve your desired outcomes.

53
00:02:43,621 --> 00:02:47,041
Second, by consistently
studying your input and output data,

54
00:02:47,041 --> 00:02:49,752
you can't help but feel the customer
pain points,

55
00:02:49,752 --> 00:02:52,547
which forces you to develop ways
to fix them.

56
00:02:52,547 --> 00:02:54,215
This continuous stream of that data

57
00:02:54,215 --> 00:02:57,218
becomes the raw material
that will spur innovation.

58
00:02:57,468 --> 00:02:59,429
But now let's
get started with session one.